{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbgnDrgiFDN9emm2Ty81","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015PA6YXDdsdhEVw79VXGvj2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; cat > /tmp/rr.json <<'EOF'\n{\"status\": \"refused\",\n \"task\": \"Melting point of n-eicosane (C20H42) with MACE-MPA-0, two-phase coexistence, classical_md production preset, T_guess 310 K\",\n \"reason\": \"Premise check failed: the property is not well-defined for this model. n-eicosane melts when the weak van der Waals (dispersion) forces between chains give way, so T_m depends almost entirely on how the model treats interactions between molecules. MACE-MPA-0 is trained on MPtrj+sAlex: inorganic crystals, PBE/PBE+U with no dispersion correction, 6 A cutoff. Molecular organic crystals like paraffins are outside that domain, and the reference DFT it was fit to does not include the physics that sets T_m. Direct test (scripts/dimer.py; two all-trans C20H42 chains relaxed with MACE-MPA-0 in float64, 62 atoms each, stacked face-to-face): E_int = -1.95 / -2.61 / -2.52 / -2.16 / -1.76 / -1.18 / -0.47 / 0.00 eV at separations of 3.6 / 3.9 / 4.2 / 4.5 / 4.8 / 5.2 / 6.0 / 8.0 A. The minimum is about -2.6 eV (about -60 kcal/mol, about 3 kcal/mol per carbon) at 3.9 A. In that geometry the facing H atoms are only about 2.1 A apart, where real chains should already be repelling each other. The attraction also drops to exactly zero beyond the cutoff, so the real long-range r^-6 tail is missing. This binding is far stronger than the near-zero binding that dispersion-free PBE gives for alkanes, and as far as I know it is also stronger than dispersion-corrected estimates for parallel alkane chains, which are roughly 1 kcal/mol per CH2 (my estimate, no source checked). So the binding comes from the model extrapolating outside its training data, not from physics. A coexistence T_m would mostly reflect this artificial binding. If it came out near 310 K that would be luck, not validation, and no error bar could cover a systematic error like this. A fair validation would need a paraffin melting point recovered with the same model, and the dimer test already shows the model fails before that point. D3 (torch-dftd) is not installed here, and adding D3 on top of a model that already overbinds would double-count the attraction anyway. Recommendation: use a force field fitted to condensed-phase organics (e.g. L-OPLS/TraPPE-UA for n-alkanes) or an MLIP trained on dispersion-corrected molecular-crystal data (e.g. MACE-OFF), and check it first on a known paraffin T_m (n-octadecane 301 K / n-eicosane 310 K from /data/calibration/tier1.json). No melting point is reported, so there is no number to attach an uncertainty to.\",\n \"evidence\": {\"dimer_scan_eV\": {\"3.6\": -1.9527, \"3.9\": -2.6051, \"4.2\": -2.5237, \"4.5\": -2.1584, \"4.8\": -1.7616, \"5.2\": -1.1826, \"6.0\": -0.4702, \"8.0\": 0.0, \"12.0\": 0.0}, \"model\": \"MACE-MPA-0 medium (mace-torch 0.3.15)\", \"experimental_Tm_K\": \"310.0 +/- 1.0 (NIST WebBook, via /data/calibration/tier1.json)\"}}\nEOF\npython -c \"import json;json.load(open('/tmp/rr.json'))\" && ./board post run_report @/tmp/rr.json --parent 765d9d39-881b-457f-a128-7b982d529ac5 --fulfills 765d9d39-881b-457f-a128-7b982d529ac5:0","description":"Post refusal run report to board"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1574,"cache_read_input_tokens":24355,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1574,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8bd986f2-14ba-4780-a0dd-aea97fdb57c1","uuid":"359219dd-2a27-44a6-9ac4-74aae1403ab1","timestamp":"2026-11-01T05:52:06.468Z","request_id":"req_01